WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.568 --> 00:00:03.484
- He's an American
business magnate, lawyer,

00:00:03.484 --> 00:00:05.585
investor and philanthropist.

00:00:05.585 --> 00:00:08.130
He's vice chairman of Berkshire Hathaway

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.888
the investment company
chaired by Warren Buffett.

00:00:10.888 --> 00:00:14.832
He has a net worth of 1.3 billion dollars.

00:00:14.832 --> 00:00:16.926
He's Charlie Munger and here are his

00:00:16.926 --> 00:00:18.880
top ten rules for success.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:19.745
(airplane flying)

00:00:19.745 --> 00:00:21.535
(basketball bounce)

00:00:21.535 --> 00:00:23.535
- The other great secret

00:00:24.818 --> 00:00:28.151
is that we're good at lifelong learning.

00:00:30.222 --> 00:00:33.126
Warren is so much better in some ways

00:00:33.126 --> 00:00:35.697
in his 70s and 80s than he was younger

00:00:35.697 --> 00:00:37.780
that it's almost awesome.

00:00:39.041 --> 00:00:41.324
If you keep learning all the time you have

00:00:41.324 --> 00:00:43.005
a huge advantage.

00:00:43.005 --> 00:00:44.681
And we both just like it.

00:00:44.681 --> 00:00:46.878
- And we have a wonderful
group of friends.

00:00:46.878 --> 00:00:49.207
Really wonderful (mumbling).

00:00:49.207 --> 00:00:52.687
- Well luckily I got at a very early age

00:00:52.687 --> 00:00:55.807
the idea that the safest way to try

00:00:55.807 --> 00:00:59.856
and get what you want is to
try and deserve what you want.

00:00:59.856 --> 00:01:01.467
It's such a simple idea

00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:04.115
it's the golden rule so to speak you

00:01:04.115 --> 00:01:06.584
you want to deliver to the
world what you would buy

00:01:06.584 --> 00:01:08.254
if you were on the other end.

00:01:08.254 --> 00:01:10.453
There is no ethos

00:01:10.453 --> 00:01:14.854
in my opinion that is
better for any lawyer

00:01:14.854 --> 00:01:16.998
or any other person to have by.

00:01:16.998 --> 00:01:20.915
And by and large the
people who have this ethos

00:01:22.056 --> 00:01:25.129
when in life and they
don't win just money,

00:01:25.129 --> 00:01:27.396
just honors and emollient once

00:01:27.396 --> 00:01:30.820
they win the respect, the deserved trust

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.665
of the people they deal with.

00:01:32.665 --> 00:01:37.380
And there is huge pleasure
in life to be obtained

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:40.282
from getting deserved trust.

00:01:40.282 --> 00:01:44.616
And so and the way to get it is to deliver

00:01:44.616 --> 00:01:48.432
what you'd want to buy
if the circumstances

00:01:48.432 --> 00:01:50.020
were reversed.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.182
One of these guys at the Berkshire meeting

00:01:52.182 --> 00:01:55.641
from one of the foreign publications said

00:01:55.641 --> 00:01:58.811
quite a couple of guys in a
little place in Omaha do so

00:01:58.811 --> 00:02:01.126
much better than all these powerful minds

00:02:01.126 --> 00:02:03.043
and great institutions.

00:02:03.955 --> 00:02:06.701
And I said, "Well I
think Warren and I know

00:02:06.701 --> 00:02:10.280
"the edge of our competency
better than other people do."

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.327
And that's humility and an umbrella sense

00:02:14.327 --> 00:02:17.035
and that is a very
important thing to know.

00:02:17.035 --> 00:02:19.666
I say over and over again

00:02:19.666 --> 00:02:23.387
it's not a competency if you
don't know the edge of it.

00:02:23.387 --> 00:02:26.270
You were a disaster if you don't know

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:28.476
the edge of your own competency.

00:02:28.476 --> 00:02:32.696
Why I'm frequently says
I'd rather deal with a guy

00:02:32.696 --> 00:02:34.113
with an IQ of 130

00:02:35.018 --> 00:02:37.263
who thinks it's 125.

00:02:37.263 --> 00:02:40.785
Than the guy with the IQ
of 180 who thinks it's 200.

00:02:40.785 --> 00:02:42.326
(laughing)

00:02:42.326 --> 00:02:43.985
That second guy will kill you.

00:02:43.985 --> 00:02:45.528
(laughing)

00:02:45.528 --> 00:02:48.504
And and so it is very important to

00:02:48.504 --> 00:02:51.126
know the edge of your own competency.

00:02:51.126 --> 00:02:52.626
It's not my nature

00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:58.630
when you get little surprises
as a result of human nature

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:02.313
to spend much time feeling betrayed.

00:03:02.313 --> 00:03:04.373
I'm always want to just put my head down

00:03:04.373 --> 00:03:07.483
on it just so I don't allow myself to

00:03:07.483 --> 00:03:11.102
spend much time ever with any feelings

00:03:11.102 --> 00:03:12.102
of betrayal.

00:03:13.089 --> 00:03:15.126
So you're asking the wrong person because

00:03:15.126 --> 00:03:17.536
of a if some flickering
idea like that came to me

00:03:17.536 --> 00:03:19.696
I'd get rid of it quickly.

00:03:19.696 --> 00:03:22.530
I don't like any feeling of
being victimized I think that's

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:25.980
a counterproductive way
to think as a human being.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.397
I am not a victim

00:03:28.771 --> 00:03:30.526
I'm a survivor.

00:03:30.526 --> 00:03:34.109
I listened in law school
when some wag said

00:03:34.977 --> 00:03:36.810
a legal mind is a mind

00:03:38.086 --> 00:03:40.086
that when two things are

00:03:41.399 --> 00:03:43.606
all twisted up together

00:03:43.606 --> 00:03:46.360
and interacting it's feasible

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:50.616
to think responsibly about
one thing and not the other.

00:03:50.616 --> 00:03:52.962
Well I could see from that
one sentence that that was

00:03:52.962 --> 00:03:57.283
perfectly ridiculous and
it pushed me further into

00:03:57.283 --> 00:03:59.076
my natural drift.

00:03:59.076 --> 00:04:02.493
Which was into learning all the big ideas

00:04:03.384 --> 00:04:06.498
and all the big disciplines
so I wouldn't be a perfect

00:04:06.498 --> 00:04:09.748
damn fool who was trying to think about

00:04:10.791 --> 00:04:13.301
one aspect of something
that couldn't be removed

00:04:13.301 --> 00:04:15.170
from the totality of the situation

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:17.337
in a constructive fashion.

00:04:19.449 --> 00:04:22.816
And what I noted since
the really big ideas

00:04:22.816 --> 00:04:26.254
carry 95% of freight there wasn't

00:04:26.254 --> 00:04:30.538
at all hard for me to
pick up all the big ideas

00:04:30.538 --> 00:04:34.039
in all the disciplines and
make them a standard part

00:04:34.039 --> 00:04:35.872
of my mental routines.

00:04:37.473 --> 00:04:39.341
Once you have the ideas of course

00:04:39.341 --> 00:04:40.907
they're no good if you don't practice,

00:04:40.907 --> 00:04:43.146
if you don't practice you lose it.

00:04:43.146 --> 00:04:46.826
So I went through life
constantly practicing this

00:04:46.826 --> 00:04:49.149
Malo disciplinary approach.

00:04:49.149 --> 00:04:51.950
Well I can't tell you
what that's done for me.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:55.416
It's it's it's made life more fun,

00:04:55.416 --> 00:04:57.490
it's made me more constructive,

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:59.157
it's made me more helpful to others,

00:04:59.157 --> 00:05:01.246
it's made me enormously rich

00:05:01.246 --> 00:05:02.477
you name it.

00:05:02.477 --> 00:05:05.176
That attitude really helps.

00:05:05.176 --> 00:05:07.173
We have to deal in things that

00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:10.156
we're capable of understanding.

00:05:10.156 --> 00:05:15.022
And then once we're over
that filter we have to have

00:05:15.022 --> 00:05:19.615
a business with some
intrinsic characteristics

00:05:19.615 --> 00:05:22.791
that give it a durable
competitive advantage.

00:05:22.791 --> 00:05:26.821
And then of course we would
vastly prefer a management

00:05:26.821 --> 00:05:30.146
in place with a lot of
integrity and talent.

00:05:30.146 --> 00:05:34.070
And finally no matter how wonderful it is

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:36.374
it's not worth an infinite price.

00:05:36.374 --> 00:05:39.957
So we have to have a
price that makes sense

00:05:41.366 --> 00:05:44.356
and gives a margin of safety considering

00:05:44.356 --> 00:05:46.973
the natural vicissitudes of life.

00:05:46.973 --> 00:05:50.089
That's a very simple set of ideas

00:05:50.089 --> 00:05:54.241
and the reason that our
ideas have not spread faster

00:05:54.241 --> 00:05:56.300
is they're too simple.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.800
The professional classes can't

00:05:59.943 --> 00:06:03.416
justify their existence if
that's all I have to say.

00:06:03.416 --> 00:06:05.832
We have this system
where we translate very

00:06:05.832 --> 00:06:09.022
trustworthy people and
give them enormous trust.

00:06:09.022 --> 00:06:12.122
And as they practice that
trust they get more confirmed

00:06:12.122 --> 00:06:14.161
in being trustworthy.

00:06:14.161 --> 00:06:16.638
And eventually we've got
this seamless web of trust

00:06:16.638 --> 00:06:20.692
which is incredibly
efficient and very useful.

00:06:20.692 --> 00:06:24.275
And by the way this is
not just my doctrine

00:06:26.459 --> 00:06:29.932
there's a doctrine economics
that tries to explain

00:06:29.932 --> 00:06:32.806
why firms come into existence.

00:06:32.806 --> 00:06:35.392
And firms come into existence
because a lot of people

00:06:35.392 --> 00:06:38.356
who trust one another
operating within a firm

00:06:38.356 --> 00:06:40.886
are more efficient than
they would be if they were

00:06:40.886 --> 00:06:43.202
a bunch of independent proprietors.

00:06:43.202 --> 00:06:46.202
I'm really following a very key idea

00:06:47.797 --> 00:06:50.399
of the greatest lawyer of antiquity.

00:06:50.399 --> 00:06:54.431
Marcus Tullius Cicero
and Cicero is famous for

00:06:54.431 --> 00:06:56.931
saying a man who doesn't know

00:06:58.091 --> 00:07:00.177
what happened before he's born

00:07:00.177 --> 00:07:02.520
goes through life like a child.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.451
That is a very correct idea of Cicero's

00:07:05.451 --> 00:07:08.368
and he's right to ridicule somebody

00:07:09.515 --> 00:07:13.031
so foolish as not to know what
happened before he was born.

00:07:13.031 --> 00:07:17.731
But if you generalize
Cicero as I think one should

00:07:17.731 --> 00:07:21.181
there are all these other
things that you should know

00:07:21.181 --> 00:07:23.010
in addition to history.

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:25.803
And those other things are the big ideas

00:07:25.803 --> 00:07:27.938
and all the other (mumbling) ones.

00:07:27.938 --> 00:07:29.773
And it doesn't help you
just to know them enough

00:07:29.773 --> 00:07:32.836
so you could prattle them
back on an exam and get an A.

00:07:32.836 --> 00:07:35.897
You have to learn these
things in such a way

00:07:35.897 --> 00:07:38.525
that they're in a mental
lattice work in your head

00:07:38.525 --> 00:07:41.510
and you automatically use them
for the rest of your life.

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:45.400
If you do that I solemnly
promise you that one day

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:47.801
you'll be walking down the street and

00:07:47.801 --> 00:07:49.741
you look to your right
and left you'll think,

00:07:49.741 --> 00:07:53.235
my heavenly days I'm now one of the

00:07:53.235 --> 00:07:57.062
few most competent people
of my whole age covert.

00:07:57.062 --> 00:07:59.561
Where have I have not made our way in life

00:07:59.561 --> 00:08:02.921
by making successful
macroeconomic predictions

00:08:02.921 --> 00:08:06.325
and betting on our conclusions.

00:08:06.325 --> 00:08:07.658
Our system is to

00:08:09.651 --> 00:08:12.038
swim as competently as we can

00:08:12.038 --> 00:08:14.276
and sometimes the tide will be with us

00:08:14.276 --> 00:08:16.442
and sometimes it will be against us.

00:08:16.442 --> 00:08:19.085
But by and large we don't much
bother with trying to predict

00:08:19.085 --> 00:08:22.623
the tides because we plan to play the game

00:08:22.623 --> 00:08:23.969
for a long time.

00:08:23.969 --> 00:08:27.927
I recommend to you all of
exactly the same attitude.

00:08:27.927 --> 00:08:30.894
It's kind of a snare and a delusion to

00:08:30.894 --> 00:08:33.394
outguess macroeconomic cycles.

00:08:35.138 --> 00:08:37.464
Very few people do it successfully

00:08:37.464 --> 00:08:40.395
and some of them do it by accident.

00:08:40.395 --> 00:08:44.478
And the game is that tough
why not adopt the other system

00:08:44.478 --> 00:08:47.561
of swimming as competently as you can

00:08:49.009 --> 00:08:51.479
and figuring that over a long life

00:08:51.479 --> 00:08:55.662
you'll have your share of
good tides and bad tides.

00:08:55.662 --> 00:08:58.892
Life will have terrible
blows, horrible blows,

00:08:58.892 --> 00:08:59.975
unfair blows.

00:09:02.545 --> 00:09:03.962
It doesn't matter

00:09:04.986 --> 00:09:07.148
and some people recover and others don't.

00:09:07.148 --> 00:09:11.569
And and there I think the
attitude of (mumbling)

00:09:11.569 --> 00:09:13.819
is the best he thought that

00:09:15.631 --> 00:09:18.425
every missed chance in
life was an opportunity

00:09:18.425 --> 00:09:19.973
to behave well.

00:09:19.973 --> 00:09:22.189
Every missed chance in
life was an opportunity

00:09:22.189 --> 00:09:23.726
to learn something.

00:09:23.726 --> 00:09:27.898
And that your duty was not
to be submerged in self-pity

00:09:27.898 --> 00:09:30.565
but to utilize the terrible blow

00:09:32.553 --> 00:09:33.742
and construct a finish.

00:09:33.742 --> 00:09:35.172
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:09:35.172 --> 00:09:38.010
I made this video because
Dom Aviation channel

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:38.918
asked me to.

00:09:38.918 --> 00:09:40.732
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you

00:09:40.732 --> 00:09:43.270
want me to profile next leave
it in the comments below

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:44.856
and I'll see what I can do.

00:09:44.856 --> 00:09:47.252
I'd also love to know
which of Charlie Munger's

00:09:47.252 --> 00:09:50.942
top ten rules had an
impact on you the most.

00:09:50.942 --> 00:09:52.874
Leave it in the comments
below and I'm going to

00:09:52.874 --> 00:09:53.994
join in the discussion.

00:09:53.994 --> 00:09:55.658
Thank you guys so much for watching

00:09:55.658 --> 00:09:58.908
keep to believe, and I'll see you soon.

